QUICraft patch for Netty netty-4.2.19.Final (commit 64cc10f38ea5f5bd7eae48507817c66680d0afdc),
on top of 0001 and 0002: a server that accepted 0-RTT reads early stream data before the
handshake completes.

Two things kept a server from seeing 0-RTT stream data before the client's Finished arrived,
one round trip after the data itself (measured through a 200 ms RTT relay: first reply 2.03 RTT
after the client's Initial, against 1.11 RTT once both are fixed):

1. A server QuicChannel became active, and so started reading streams (autoRead), only once
   quiche reported the connection established. handlePendingChannelActive now also activates it
   while quiche reports accepted early data (quiche_conn_is_in_early_data).
2. The client's 0-RTT packet usually arrives right behind its Initial, before the server's
   handshake has installed the 0-RTT key: Netty runs the server's TLS work as an SSL task after
   quiche_conn_recv. quiche buffers the packet and decrypts it in its next send(), which follows
   that task. Nothing looked for readable streams after that send until the client's next packet
   arrived. After each connectionSend(...) that can follow an SSL task (the same four places as
   0002), a server in early data now queues a task on its event loop that runs
   processReceived(...) (channelActive, readable streams) and sends again.

SslHandshakeCompletionEvent still fires only when the handshake has completed, so an application
can tell early data from confirmed data. QUICraft acts on early data only with a single-use token
(docs/protocol.md §8). Servers that don't enable early data are unaffected: quiche never reports
early data for them.

Applied by natives/build-netty-quic.sh with `git apply`, which ignores this text.

